ity: **-2** (long-duration pipeline; $1.525B floating-rate debt; rising 10-yr)\n- Dollar sensitivity: **-1** (mostly US sales; modest EU exposure)\n- Liquidity/biotech-risk-appetite: **-1** (Phase-3 pipeline; 12.4% short interest)\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: **+1** (healthcare defensive)\n- Commodity: **0**\n\n### 1-4 Macro Alignment\nRegime-weighted expectation \u2248 (0.35\u00d7-1)+(0.30\u00d7-1)+(0.25\u00d7-1)+(0.10\u00d7+1) \u2192 **modest headwind**. At 10-yr 4.74% (rising), the equity discount rate applicable to a 2028-2030 pipeline payoff is high. **Verdict: HEADWIND \u2014 conviction -0.5 steps; split entry mandatory.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\n**Relevant base rates:**\n- Phase-3 CNS/psychiatric drug \u2192 approval: **~45-50%** (industry aggregate ~50-60%; CNS lower) [Estimate \u2014 benchmark]\n- Post-positive Phase-2 with Breakthrough designation \u2192 Phase-3 success: **~55-65%** [Estimate]\n- $1-2.5B pharma M&A achieving ROIC > WACC: **~40%** [Estimate]\n- Our alixorexton PoS (55%) is **1.1-1.2\u00d7 the CNS base rate** \u2014 no base-rate red flag on our own model.\n\n**Market's implicit base-rate breach [Layer 1.5-3 Red Flag]:** The market prices the alixorexton/pipeline option at ~$4.5B (market EV $9.14B less our core EV $4.6B). At 4.5\u00d7 peak-sales and 4-year discounting, this implies a risked peak >$1.6B \u2014 i.e. a $1.2B peak at ~130% PoS (impossible), or $2.0-2.7B peak at 50-75% PoS. **The market's valuation embeds a success profile 1.5-1.8\u00d7 our base. Per Rule 1.5-3, this requires additional evidence; none exists pre-Phase-3. Verdict: the market, not our model, is the outlier.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n### 2-1 Understanding\nAlkermes sells four promoted neuroscience drugs (schizophrenia, bipolar depression, addiction, narcolepsy) plus earns royalties on partnered J&J long-acting antipsychotics. Its strategic bet is the orexin-agonist platform (wakefulness) across narcolepsy, idiopathic hypersomnia, ADHD, and fatigue. Understandability: **Medium-High** \u2014 commercial model is clear; pipeline value is option-like.\n\n### 2-2 Moat Assessment (0-21)\n| Moat type | Score | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (LUMRYZ REMS/oxybate delivery IP, orexin composition) | 2 | Widening |\n| Switching costs (LUMRYZ once-nightly lock-in; 3,600+ patients) | 2 | Widening |\n| Network effects | 0 | Neutral |\n| Cost advantage (LAI manufacturing scale) | 2 | Stable |\n| Economies of scale (CNS salesforce + manufacturing) | 2 | Stable |\n| Regulatory barriers (sodium oxybate REMS, Schedule III) | 2 | Stable |\n| Data moat (real-world LAI/oxybate data) | 1 | Widening |\n| **Total** | **11/21** | **Narrow moat, widening on sleep franchise; narrowing risk on VIVITROL generic 2027** |\n\n### 2-3 Lynch Classification\n**Fast Grower** with a Turnaround inflection (2022\u21922023 profitability). Key checkpoints: growth is real (LUMRYZ +$72M in first partial quarter; LYBALVI +32% YoY Q1-26; TRx +21%); sustainability requires Phase-3 results; balance-sheet leverage is new.\n\n### 2-4 Financial Strength Scorecard\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR (5-yr, FY20-FY25) | +7.3% | [Arithmetic] |\n| FY25 operating margin | 17.2% | [Arithmetic] |\n| FY25 OCF / FCF | $0.521B / $0.480B | [Actual] SEC |\n| FCF conversion (FCF/NI) | ~2.0\u00d7 (NI $242M, FCF $480M \u2014 SBC distortion) | [Arithmetic] |\n| Normalized ROIC FY25 | ~14% pre-Avadel; ~8-9% post-Avadel | [Estimate] |\n| Net debt at T0 | ~$0.95B ($1.525B debt \u2212 $0.538B cash/inv.) | [Estimate] |\n| Interest coverage (normalized EBIT/interest) | $245M / ~$100M \u2248 2.5\u00d7 | [Estimate] |\n| Piotroski F-score | 5/7 available checks | [Arithmetic] |\n| Accruals ratio | -0.12 (low accrual risk) | [Arithmetic] |\n\nVerdict: **Good** \u2014 strong cash generation; leverage is manageable (~1.3\u00d7 EBITDA) but new.\n\n### 2-5 Capital Allocation Scorecard (0-15)\n1. Buyback efficiency: